eld/validate]} {:db/id "q-123003" :question/id #uuid "4988074d-c4b5-57bf-b07b-239bfc1142f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123003" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-10,\\,y=4{t}^{6}+5.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=12{t}^{4},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=12{t}^{4},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-10)=2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(12{t}^{4})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{48{t}^{3}}{2t} \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=1}=24\\cdot {1}^{2}=24.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-123003/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-123003"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352392/field-1/8609e6aeb86d75e4499c738639006d16563c2a05081e9ddf79e7750dcb2fadb1" :answer/id #uuid "cc8fb99d-5637-5eec-8c53-c9cad261ff6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6e^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352392/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9cfa5fd6-e542-5ee3-a18e-d3564431c796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352392/field-1/8609e6aeb86d75e4499c738639006d16563c2a05081e9ddf79e7750dcb2fadb1" :answer-field/choices ["q-352392/field-1/8609e6aeb86d75e4499c738639006d16563c2a05081e9ddf79e7750dcb2fadb1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352392" :question/id #uuid "f23207c5-dc3b-502d-9895-716f52882c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-352392" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={e}^{2t},\\,y={e}^{6t}.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{4t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3{e}^{4t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3{e}^{4t})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{4t}}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=6{e}^{2(1)}=6{e}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-352392/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352392"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
